@verbatim
<p>Finding your writing voice is often described as if it were a hidden object. Search long enough, the story goes, and one day you will uncover a tone that is unmistakably yours. That makes voice sound precious and mysterious. It is usually more ordinary than that. Voice is the pattern of choices you make when you are trying to say something true.</p>
<p>Those choices include what you notice, which details you keep, how directly you make a claim, where you allow a sentence to pause, and what you refuse to pretend you know. A voice cannot be borrowed whole because it grows from attention. You can learn from other writers, and you should. But the part that readers recognize comes from returning to your own questions often enough to develop a way of answering them.</p>
<p>The exercises below are designed to make that process visible. They are not a test of whether you are a real writer. They are small ways to hear your natural language, see your habits on the page, and make more deliberate decisions about what stays.</p>
<h2>1. Write toward a subject you actually care about</h2>
<p>Voice has trouble appearing when the subject is borrowed. If you are writing what you think a writer should write, the page quickly fills with phrases you have heard before. Choose a subject that gives you a real reaction: a book that annoyed you, a family saying you cannot forget, a tool that changed how you work, a place you know too well, or a question you keep circling without a neat answer.</p>
<p>Set a timer for fifteen minutes and write without looking for a thesis. Begin with “I keep thinking about...” and follow the thought. Do not explain the whole world. Stay close to one scene, memory, object, or disagreement. The useful material is often in the sentence you nearly remove because it feels too particular.</p>
<p>Patrick’s <a href="/books/blurred-lines">Blurred Lines</a> begins with questions of culture and identity that cannot be handled honestly from a distance. That is a useful model for any writer: start where your own experience, reading, or observation gives you something real to examine.</p>
<h2>2. Make a list of sentences you wish you had written</h2>
<p>Reading widely does not erase your voice. It gives you more ways to hear language. Keep a small file of sentences that stop you. Do not collect them because they sound impressive. Collect them because they show you a move you want to understand. Maybe a writer begins quietly and makes a sharp turn. Maybe a description uses one precise noun instead of five adjectives. Maybe a plain sentence carries more feeling than a dramatic one.</p>
<p>After collecting ten examples, write beside each one: what is this sentence doing? Is it naming a tension, changing pace, making an image, admitting uncertainty, or placing two ideas in conflict? You are not trying to steal the sentence. You are learning to recognize craft decisions.</p>
<picture><source srcset="/images/blog/writing-voice-books-f44e9b62f9d7d0cf.webp" type="image/webp"/><img src="/images/blog/writing-voice-books-ddea3854679262f3.jpg" alt="Stack of well-used books on a desk, ready for close reading" loading="lazy" decoding="async"/></picture>
<p>Then try an imitation exercise. Take the shape of one sentence, not its subject, and use it to write about your own material. If a writer uses a short sentence after a long one, try that rhythm. If they open with a concrete object, open with an object from your life. The moment your subject becomes specific, the borrowed frame begins to change.</p>
<h2>3. Tell the same small story three different ways</h2>
<p>Choose a minor event: missing a train, receiving a strange message, burning dinner, moving a desk, or finding an old photograph. Tell it first as a text message to a friend. Then write it as the opening of an essay. Finally, write it as a scene in a short story. Give yourself ten minutes for each version.</p>
<p>Read the three versions together. Notice what remains. You may find that you always begin with a detail, that humor arrives when you are uncomfortable, or that you are drawn to the part other people would skip. Those recurring choices are more useful than a label such as lyrical or conversational. They show you what your mind reaches for when the form changes.</p>
<p>This exercise also makes an important point: voice is not the same as format. A writer can sound different in a review, a letter, and a story because each one asks for a different degree of explanation. The thread is not a fixed set of mannerisms. It is a consistent quality of attention.</p>
<h2>4. Replace general language with what you can see</h2>
<p>When writers worry about sounding original, they often reach for large abstract words. The result can sound polished at first and empty a minute later. “It was a meaningful experience” tells a reader almost nothing. “The bus driver waited while I searched every pocket for a fare card” gives the reader a situation, pressure, and a person.</p>
<p>Take one paragraph of your own writing and underline the broadest nouns and verbs. Words such as thing, nice, difficult, important, felt, went, or changed are not forbidden. They are signals to ask a better question. What thing? Difficult in what way? What changed on the surface of the day? Add one concrete detail that only you would have chosen.</p>
<picture><source srcset="/images/blog/writing-voice-notes-5c3752808d80ca4e.webp" type="image/webp"/><img src="/images/blog/writing-voice-notes-78231b0822a41ffa.jpg" alt="Person writing a daily entry in an open notebook at a wooden desk" loading="lazy" decoding="async"/></picture>
<p>Specificity does not mean recording every fact. It means choosing the fact that carries the most weight. In <a href="/blog/creator-practice/build-creative-practice">How to Build a Creative Practice That Lasts</a>, the useful unit of work is deliberately small: a marked page, a rough sketch, a next question. Details like these turn ambition into something a person can actually begin.</p>
<h2>5. Read your work aloud and listen for the stiff parts</h2>
<p>Your ear catches what your eye excuses. Read a page aloud at a normal speaking pace. Mark the sentences where you run out of breath, where the rhythm becomes flat, or where you sound unlike yourself. Do not rush to make everything shorter. Some long sentences need room. The point is to hear whether the sentence is carrying its own weight.</p>
<p>Pay attention to the words you would never use in conversation with an intelligent friend. They may still be the right words, but they deserve a reason to be there. Often the stiff passage is trying too hard to prove that it belongs in print. Replace it with the clearest version of what you mean, then read it aloud again.</p>
<picture><source srcset="/images/blog/writing-voice-reading-aloud-ff72fbe7228ae718.webp" type="image/webp"/><img src="/images/blog/writing-voice-reading-aloud-ac8213beff61d5ed.jpg" alt="A stack of books on a desk for reading and reflection" loading="lazy" decoding="async"/></picture>
<p>Reading aloud is especially helpful after you have put a draft away for a day. Distance weakens the attachment to the first phrasing and lets you hear the reader’s experience more clearly. A draft is not a verdict. It is material you can shape.</p>
<h2>6. Write a page you do not intend to publish</h2>
<p>Many writers become guarded the moment they imagine an audience. They begin arranging the furniture before there is a room. Write one private page about something you are not trying to make useful, impressive, or marketable. It can be a complaint, a memory, a letter, a description of a room, or a question you do not know how to answer.</p>
<p>Later, circle the lines that feel alive. The goal is not to publish the private page. It is to notice what changed when you stopped performing. Did your sentences become shorter? Did you make better jokes? Did you admit more uncertainty? Did you reach for images instead of explanations? Those are clues about the conditions that help your voice arrive.</p>
<p>Private writing also gives you a place to practice honesty without confusing honesty with disclosure. You do not owe every reader every detail of your life. You do owe the work enough truth that the reader can feel a person behind the sentences.</p>
<h2>7. Revise for precision, not personality</h2>
<p>Voice is not created by sprinkling quirks over a clean draft. Too many familiar phrases, exaggerated certainty, or decorative fragments can become a costume. Revision works better when you ask practical questions: Is this what I mean? Is there a simpler word? Does this paragraph move? Have I made a claim the example cannot support?</p>
<p>Try a two-pass revision. In the first pass, remove anything that only repeats a point you have already made. In the second, add one detail, question, or qualification where the draft becomes vague. Cutting gives the prose shape. Adding gives it life. The balance is where a more recognizable voice begins to emerge.</p>
<p>This is close to the argument in <a href="/books/iq-an-overrated-quotient">IQ: An Overrated Quotient</a>: imagination becomes useful when it meets patient structure. A distinctive voice needs both. You need enough freedom to make an unusual connection and enough discipline to make the connection clear to someone else.</p>
<h2>Keep a small record of what sounds like you</h2>
<p>After each writing session, write down one thing you noticed about your language. It might be “I explain too soon,” “my best line came after the example,” “I like short openings,” or “I keep avoiding the hard noun.” This record is not a scorecard. It is a way to make your own patterns easier to see over time.</p>
<p>Return to an old piece after a month and ask what still feels true. Keep the line that surprised you. Cut the sentence that is only copying a mood. Notice where your curiosity is becoming more precise. That is more durable than trying to sound like a particular kind of writer.</p>
<p>Do not mistake consistency for sameness. Your voice may be more spare in an essay than in a journal entry. It may become more direct when you write about work and more exploratory when you write about memory. That variation is not a failure to settle on an identity. It is evidence that you are paying attention to the task in front of you. The goal is not to make every piece sound identical. It is to make each piece feel chosen rather than assembled from phrases that could belong to anyone.</p>
<p>A useful final exercise is to compare two pages written six months apart. Read them without deciding which is better. Instead, notice what has become clearer. Perhaps you name the real subject sooner. Perhaps you trust a simple sentence. Perhaps you are willing to leave a question open. Those changes are part of a voice developing in public and in private, one honest decision at a time.</p>
<p>For writers who also want to share work publicly, <a href="/blog/creator-practice/how-to-become-an-influencer">How to Become an Influencer Without Losing Your Voice</a> offers a useful companion idea: attention becomes more valuable when it is earned through a clear point of view rather than a borrowed persona. The same principle applies on the page. Readers return when they can trust that your language is serving the subject, not merely asking to be noticed.</p>
<h2>How Patrick Okeke’s books support this work</h2>
<p>Writing voice is built through the subjects that keep asking for your attention. Patrick Okeke’s books give readers several places to begin: culture and identity in <a href="/books/blurred-lines">Blurred Lines</a>, trust and persuasion in <a href="/books/boosting-the-size-of-sale">Boosting the Size of Sale</a>, changing leadership in <a href="/books/ceos-of-tiktok">CEOs of TikTok</a>, and the meeting point of creative and technical thought in <a href="/books/iq-an-overrated-quotient">IQ: An Overrated Quotient</a>.</p>
<p>Use a book as a prompt, not an answer key. Underline one idea that unsettles or interests you, then write for ten minutes about where it touches your own experience. A writing voice grows when you have something worth responding to and enough patience to find the sentence that is actually yours.</p>
<section class="article-faq"><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
<div><h3>How long does it take to find your writing voice?</h3>
<p>There is no finish line. Your voice becomes easier to recognize as you write more, read closely, and notice which choices you make repeatedly. A few weeks of regular practice can reveal useful patterns, but the work keeps changing as your interests and experience deepen.</p>
</div>
<div><h3>Can I copy writers I admire while learning?</h3>
<p>Yes, as an exercise. Imitation can teach you how a sentence is built, where a paragraph turns, or how a writer handles detail. The important part is to move on from the exercise and write about a subject that asks for your own judgment.</p>
</div>
<div><h3>What if my writing voice sounds different in different formats?</h3>
<p>That is normal. A note to a friend, a book review, and a formal essay have different jobs. Voice is not one unchanging sound. It is the recognizable quality that remains when your language adapts to the reader, subject, and form.</p>
</div>
<div><h3>Should I try to make my writing sound more sophisticated?</h3>
<p>Only when the idea needs more precision. Sophistication is not a pile of expensive words. Clear language, concrete detail, and an honest rhythm usually make a stronger impression than sentences that seem to be auditioning for a larger room.</p>
</div>
